## P19 basic consumer probes — 2026-10-25

- Added and committed the Java 5.5.1/Rust direct consumer-offset and pull probes in `cb351d8ae6a67b9bdb4cbf9fdacd497602e0b9a4` (`Phase 19: add consumer offset and pull probes`). `CONS-001-query-offset`, `CONS-002-pull-found`, `CONS-003-pull-no-new-msg`, and `CONS-004-update-offset` each pass on a fresh basic-profile Broker project with `source_code_dirty=false`; clean evidence IDs are `20261025T025233Z`, `20261025T031409Z`, `20261025T031529Z`, and `20261025T031646Z`, respectively.
- The found-pull probe compares response status, offsets, broker ID, and raw message bytes, and checks that the seeded `pull-seed` body is present. The empty-pull probe requires `NO_NEW_MSG` with an empty body. Both clients set the same subscription sysflag using the pinned Java helper semantics.
- Diagnostic runs are retained but not referenced as passing evidence: the first pull attempt lacked the subscription flag and received Broker error 24; a committed-revision `CONS-004` attempt timed out on its first Java update RPC, cleaned its owned topic and Compose project, and passed on a later fresh project.
- The fail-closed coverage check now reports 26 missing IDs and exits before starting Docker. The other 26 local live cases remain pending. This does not close the two-Broker/TLS/ACL/transaction/request-reply/admin/stress matrices, broad P18-V01 audit, MAN-01..05 owner inputs, or remote Docker CI.
- Follow-up commits preserve the existing Phase 19 commits; no existing commit was amended or rewritten. `--check-all` is expected to fail until all 37 live cases have runners.

